The helper receives the result slice and keeps adding values in the middle of the two recursive calls. Passing the same slice through the whole walk avoids building separate results for the left and right sides.

The function uses the field names from the supplied Go node template. It does not change any child pointers.

```go
// TreeNode definition for a binary tree node.
// type TreeNode struct {
// 	Val int
// 	Left *TreeNode
// 	Right *TreeNode
// }

func getInorder(root *TreeNode) []int {
    result := make([]int, 0)

    var walk func(*TreeNode)
    walk = func(node *TreeNode) {
        if node == nil {
            return
        }

        walk(node.Left)
        result = append(result, node.Val)
        walk(node.Right)
    }

    walk(root)
    return result
}
```

If the input is `nil`, the helper returns immediately and the function returns an empty slice. A tree containing only right children is also fine, because every left call simply receives `nil`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the call stack, where `h` is the height of the tree. The output slice stores `n` values.

A stack is a simple way to remember the ancestors while moving left. Every node is pushed before its left subtree is handled. When there is no more left child, pop a node, add it to the answer, and continue from its right child.

The empty tree is handled because the loop only runs while there is a current node or a node waiting in the stack.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> getInorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode current = root;

        while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (current != null) {
                stack.push(current);
                current = current.left;
            }

            current = stack.pop();
            result.add(current.data);
            current = current.right;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Dry run for a tree shaped like `1` with left child `2` and right child `3`: push `1`, then `2`, pop `2`, record it, pop `1`, record it, and finally process `3`. The result is `[2, 1, 3]`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(h)`, where `h` is the tree height. A very skewed tree can make the stack hold `O(n)` nodes.

Morris traversal keeps the result in left, root, right order without recursion or a stack. When a node has a left child, find the rightmost node in that left subtree. Its empty right pointer becomes a temporary link back to the current node. On the second visit, remove that link and record the current value.

For a tree with nodes `1`, `2`, and `3`, the temporary link lets traversal return from `2`'s subtree to `1`. The links are always removed before moving on, so the input tree is restored.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getInorder(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> result;
        TreeNode* current = root;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->left == nullptr) {
                result.push_back(current->data);
                current = current->right;
            } else {
                TreeNode* predecessor = current->left;

                while (predecessor->right != nullptr &&
                       predecessor->right != current) {
                    predecessor = predecessor->right;
                }

                if (predecessor->right == nullptr) {
                    predecessor->right = current;
                    current = current->left;
                } else {
                    predecessor->right = nullptr;
                    result.push_back(current->data);
                    current = current->right;
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Each link is followed only a small number of times.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the returned list.
